    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe endpoint wiring, entity field, schema changes across all four DB dialects, and OpenAPI doc are all placed naturally and follow the conventions here. The problem is one structural duplication that this codebase already has an established idiom for avoiding.\n\n**Duplicated city-counting logic between `AssessCityCapacity` and `EnsureCityCapacity`.**\n\n`AssessCityCapacity.service` copies, verbatim, the counting-and-city-matching body of `EnsureCityCapacity.service`:\n\n```java\nlong inCity = ownerRepository.findAll().stream()\n    .filter(existing -> city != null && city.equals(existing.getCity()))\n    .count();\n```\n\nplus the `CITY_CAPACITY = 50` constant. These are now the two halves of the *same* city-capacity rule (hard reject at 50, warn at 40\u201349) with the shared \"how do we count owners in a city\" logic living in two places. If the matching rule ever changes \u2014 case-insensitive city match, excluding soft-deleted owners, trimming whitespace \u2014 the warning and the rejection will silently drift apart, and the stated invariant in the Javadoc (\"matches cities by exact value, exactly as the hard rejection does\") stops being guaranteed by construction.\n\nThis is exactly the situation the sibling pair already solved the right way: `AssessBulkSignup` (the warning) and `EnsureDailyLimit` (the hard limit) both route their per-day counting through the shared `BusinessDays` helper, and `AssessBulkSignup`'s own Javadoc calls this out \u2014 \"the same single source of truth \u2026 so the two per-day rules cannot drift apart.\" The city-capacity pair should mirror that.\n\nSuggested fix: extract the shared count (and the `CITY_CAPACITY` constant) into one place \u2014 e.g. a small `CityCapacity` helper with `countInCity(OwnerRepository, String city)` and the limit constant \u2014 and have both `EnsureCityCapacity` and `AssessCityCapacity` call it, the same way the daily-limit pair leans on `BusinessDays`. That keeps the hard limit and the warning anchored to a single definition of \"owners in a city.\"",
